  "encoding_scheme": {
    "top": "technicalFields encoded as 2-out-of-N notch pairs. Position 34 is a universal 'card present' notch (notched on 99% of cards), not a code.",
    "bottom": "geographic location (state). Each state has a SINGLE primary notch position \u2014 what an operator would query with one needle. Other 'companion' positions are nearly always notched alongside the primary and likely encode within-state regions or are part of wide grouped notches (cardstock cut across 2+ adjacent positions as one rectangle). See bottom_geo_codes[state].primary vs .companions.",
    "right": "first letter of surname. The encoding is per-letter superimposed (several positions act as 'this letter is in group X' indicators); see per_letter_strong_positions. Classifier achieves ~53% top-1 accuracy (vs 4.5% chance), confirming the encoding exists but is not deterministic in the data we observed.",
    "left": "positions 2, 14, 15 correlate with the NUMBER OF CROSS-REFERENCES (entries) on the BACK of the card. Cards with any of these three notched have ~3x the back-entry count of cards with none of them notched. This likely encodes 'how connected this person is in the E.A.T. network' so operators could quickly retrieve highly-cited people. Other left positions (3-13) had weaker individual signals."
  },
